Derive coefficient uncertainty from linear observation weights (@inquiry-linear-estimator: 0, is #chapter, #hephaestus-core "Understand residual constraints and estimated scatter.lingua#r_007A3A0DF16E49979BF8C6FD9E") { r_00089F9DC6E7927277B93E8393
The shared response lesson calls slope a and intercept b. Here use β for slope and α for intercept throughout. Let x̄=Σx_i/n, d_i=x_i−x̄ and Sxx=Σd_i²>0. The projection result gives β̂=Σd_iY_i/Sxx and α̂=Ȳ−β̂x̄. Their observed versions replace Y by y. If Sxx=0 the observations cannot separate intercept and slope: numerical minimization cannot create the missing information.

Derive the formulas from the two residual constraints Σ(Y_i−α̂−β̂x_i)=0 and Σx_i(Y_i−α̂−β̂x_i)=0, rather than learning separate fitting rules. Then insert Y_i=α+βx_i+ε_i. Since Σd_i=0 and Σd_ix_i=Sxx, β̂−β=Σd_iε_i/Sxx and α̂−α=Σε_i/n−x̄Σd_iε_i/Sxx. Zero mean errors imply unbiased coefficients. No distributional shape has been invoked.

For arbitrary error covariance matrix Σ, any two linear combinations cᵀε and dᵀε have covariance cᵀΣd, by expanding the double sum. Under Σ=σ²I, the preceding weights give Var(β̂)=σ²/Sxx, Var(α̂)=σ²(1/n+x̄²/Sxx), Cov(α̂,β̂)=−x̄σ²/Sxx. The units are respectively U²/X², U² and U²/X. These are sampling variances for known σ²; substituting an estimated variance is an additional step.

Centering the coordinate to z=x−x̄ makes the new intercept α+βx̄ and its estimator Ȳ. Its covariance with β̂ is zero under the stated covariance model. Zero covariance does not generally mean independence. Rescaling z by a nonzero constant changes the slope unit and numerical variance, while leaving fitted physical responses unchanged. Reuse [Keep coordinate choices error metrics and sensitivity distinct](../../mathematics/core/Keep coordinate choices error metrics and sensitivity distinct.lingua#r_00BDB35AFC4D0B673CC82739C7) when comparing numerical sensitivity with information content.

For a linear unbiased slope estimator Σc_iY_i, unbiasedness for every α,β requires Σc_i=0 and Σc_ix_i=1. Write c_i=d_i/Sxx+v_i. The constraints imply Σv_i=Σv_id_i=0, so Σc_i²=1/Sxx+Σv_i². Under equal uncorrelated errors its variance is at least σ²/Sxx. This proves an optimality statement among these linear unbiased estimators only; it does not prove the mean model or superiority under a different loss/covariance structure.

**Work:** derive all three covariance expressions by scalar sums. Replace Σ with σ²I+τ²11ᵀ and determine which coefficient acquires extra variance. For unequal or correlated errors, return to cᵀΣd rather than reusing the equal-variance formulas. A chosen weighted objective is a mathematical metric; calling its weights inverse variances requires evidence.

